<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Derge</h1><p class="st-meta">1448 CE – 1909 CE (approx.) · capital Derge</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading kingdom of eastern Tibet (Kham), renowned for the Derge printing house and as the cradle of the 19th-century Rime religious movement.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The royal house of Derge claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gar-tongtsen">Gar Tongtsen</a>, minister of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire</a>. The family rose in the 15th century around the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakya-regime">Sakya</a> monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derge-gonchen-monastery">Lhundrup Teng</a>, which became the royal seat, and gradually extended its rule over large parts of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings combined secular and religious authority and ruled through hereditary officials over farming valleys and nomad pastures. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenpa-tsering">Tenpa Tsering</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derge-parkhang">Derge Parkhang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> house in 1729, which carved woodblocks for the Buddhist canon still used today. In the 19th century Derge patronised the non-sectarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rime-movement">Rime movement</a> of teachers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamgon-kongtrul">Jamgön Kongtrul</a>. Derge recognised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> suzerainty while remaining close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1848 Derge was occupied by the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonpo-namgyal">Gönpo Namgyal</a> of Nyarong until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa's</a> troops expelled him in 1865. In 1909 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-erfeng">Zhao Erfeng</a> deposed the king and placed Derge under direct Chinese administration.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-derge.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Derge" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: René Heise · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_Potala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1448 CE – 1909 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Derge</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tenpa Tsering</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Sakya, Nyingma, Kagyu)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Derge</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=kingdom-of-derge" aria-label="Kingdom of Derge on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7222 1020 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Derge lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7542 1276L7567 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7567 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7542 1276h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7542 1276h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=kingdom-of-derge">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

